For the Lucas numbers, W_1 = a, W_2 = bbb
It can be shown (by the same method as above) that every word with n >= 2 ends with bbb, every even word begins with bbb and every odd word begins with a.

If we define a double-b as two consecutive b's, then an n-b (n consecutive b's) contains n-1 double-b's (n >= 1)
Proof: A single-b contains no double-b's
Assume a k-b contains k-1 double-b's
(k+1)-b = (k-b)b
The new b added forms another double-b with the last b of the k-b
The other b's are unaffected, as they are not consecutive with the new b
Therefore the (k+1)-b has (k-1) + 1 = k = (k+1) - 1 b's
Therefore, by induction, an n-b has (n-1) double-b's

Every odd n combines two 3-b's to form a 6-b
The two 3-b's have 2 double-b's each, for 4 total
The 6-b has 5 double-b's
Therefore, each odd n adds one double-b on top of those in the two previous words
Therefore, N_k = N_{k-1} + N_{k-2} + mod 2 for k >= 4, N_2 = N_3 = 2
